## 🚫 Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't Create Custom CSS Classes

```css
/* ❌ Wrong - Don't create custom CSS classes */
.custom-button {
  padding: 1rem 2rem;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  background-color: var(--modus-wc-color-base-100);
}

.custom-card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
```

### ❌ Don't Use Tailwind Color Classes

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong - Don't use Tailwind color classes
<div className="bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 border-gray-200">
  Content
</div>

// ❌ Wrong - Don't use hardcoded colors
<div className="bg-white text-black">
  Content
</div>
```

### ❌ Don't Mix Custom CSS with Tailwind

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong - Don't mix custom CSS classes with Tailwind
<div className="custom-card flex items-center gap-4">Content</div>
```

## 🎯 Benefits of This Approach

### ✅ Maintainability

- **Single Source of Truth**: All styling lives with the component
- **No CSS File Hunting**: No need to search through multiple CSS files
- **Easier Refactoring**: Changes are localized to the component

### ✅ Consistency

- **Modus Color System**: All colors use approved Modus CSS variables
- **Tailwind Standards**: Consistent spacing, typography, and layout patterns
- **Theme Compatibility**: Automatic theme switching support

### ✅ Performance

- **Smaller CSS Bundle**: Minimal custom CSS reduces bundle size
- **Tree Shaking**: Unused Tailwind classes are automatically removed
- **Better Caching**: Tailwind classes are more cacheable

### ✅ Developer Experience

- **IntelliSense**: Better autocomplete for Tailwind classes
- **Documentation**: Tailwind classes are well-documented
- **Debugging**: Easier to debug styling issues

## 🎨 File Organization

### ✅ Keep globals.css Minimal

```css
/* ✅ Only essential global styles */
@import url("https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@trimble-oss/modus-icons@1.17.0/dist/field-systems/fonts/modus-icons.css");
@import "tailwindcss";

:root {
  --background: var(--modus-wc-color-base-page);
  --foreground: var(--modus-wc-color-base-content);
}

body {
  background: var(--background);
  color: var(--foreground);
  font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
}

/* Theme-based logo switching */
.nextjs-logo-light {
  display: block;
}
.nextjs-logo-dark {
  display: none;
}

[data-theme="modus-classic-dark"] .nextjs-logo-light,
[data-theme="modus-modern-dark"] .nextjs-logo-light {
  display: none;
}

[data-theme="modus-classic-dark"] .nextjs-logo-dark,
[data-theme="modus-modern-dark"] .nextjs-logo-dark {
  display: block;
}
```

### ✅ Component-Specific Styling

```tsx
// ✅ All styling in the component file
export default function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <div
      className="flex items-center justify-between p-4 rounded-lg border"
      style={{
        backgroundColor: "var(--modus-wc-color-base-page)",
        borderColor: "var(--modus-wc-color-base-200)",
        color: "var(--modus-wc-color-base-content)",
      }}
    >
      {/* Component content */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## 🎯 Summary

**Use Tailwind CSS for:**

- ✅ Layout (flexbox, grid, positioning)
- ✅ Spacing (padding, margin, gap)
- ✅ Typography (font sizes, weights, line heights)
- ✅ Responsive design (breakpoints, mobile-first)
- ✅ Transitions and animations
- ✅ Interactive states (hover, focus)

**Use Modus CSS Variables for:**

- ✅ All colors (background, text, borders)
- ✅ Theme-aware styling
- ✅ Semantic color meanings

**Avoid:**

- ❌ Custom CSS classes
- ❌ Tailwind color classes
- ❌ Hardcoded hex/RGB values
- ❌ Mixing custom CSS with Tailwind

This approach ensures maintainable, consistent, and theme-compatible styling across your Next.js application with Modus Design System.

